```java
class Singleton {
    private static final Singleton INSTANCE = new Singleton();
    private Singleton() {}
    static Singleton getInstance() { return INSTANCE; }
}
```

Simplest correct version: the JVM guarantees static field initialization happens exactly once, under an implicit lock, before any thread can observe the class. No explicit synchronization is needed at all — the classloader does it for you. The only cost is `INSTANCE` is constructed the first time anyone touches the class, even if `getInstance()` is never called — usually irrelevant, unless construction is expensive or has side effects you want strictly deferred.

```java
class Singleton {
    private static volatile Singleton instance;
    static Singleton getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            synchronized (Singleton.class) {
                if (instance == null) {
                    instance = new Singleton();
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```

Correct, but strictly more complex than the eager version, and it earns that complexity only when construction is genuinely expensive and must be deferred until first real use. See [double-checked locking explained](iv-double-checked-locking-explained) for exactly why the `volatile` here is load-bearing, not optional.

```java
enum Singleton {
    INSTANCE;
    void doSomething() { /* ... */ }
}
```

The simplest option of all: Java guarantees enum constants are constructed exactly once, thread-safely, by the classloader — for free, with one line. It's also the only common approach immune to two subtle attacks the others aren't: reflection can call a private constructor a second time via `setAccessible(true)`, and naive Java serialization can deserialize a second instance — the JVM's handling of enums specifically blocks both.
